Concise research checkpoint for Provider Composition Live Brief

# Provider Composition Live Brief — Research Checkpoint

- **Core framing:** Provider composition should be treated as a contract-first orchestration layer: normalize provider capabilities behind a stable interface, then assemble, select, or override implementations through configuration and dependency injection.
- **Why it matters:** A composed provider layer can support routing by capability, cost, latency, geography, or data-retention policy while reducing lock-in and enabling controlled migration.
- **Reliability pattern:** Production composition needs explicit health signals, timeouts, retries, circuit breakers, bulkheads, and deterministic failover. Static “provider is up” checks are insufficient; semantic or task-level quality signals are also needed.
- **Control-plane/data-plane split:** Keep provider registration, policy, credentials, and routing rules in a control plane; keep request execution and provider adapters in the data plane. This allows policy changes without rewriting adapters.
- **Testability:** Provider substitution and overrides make local, staging, and failure-mode testing practical; contract tests should verify capability parity and edge-case behavior across implementations.

## Risks / open questions
- “Composition” may mean sequential chaining, parallel fan-out/merge, fallback routing, or dependency injection; the brief should define the intended mode and scope.
- Cross-provider differences in schemas, streaming, quotas, latency, and error semantics can create silent behavior drift.
- Routing can conflict with privacy, residency, licensing, or vendor-specific retention requirements; policy precedence must be explicit.
- Need baseline metrics and acceptance thresholds for quality, cost, latency, failover recovery, and data handling.

## Recommendations / next steps
- Define a minimal provider contract with capability discovery, normalized errors, request metadata, and trace correlation.
- Start with one primary plus one fallback provider and a policy-driven router; add fan-out only after measuring value.
- Instrument per-provider success, semantic quality, p50/p95 latency, cost, quota pressure, and fallback rate.
- Validate with contract, chaos, and replay tests before enabling automatic production failover.

Sources: Microsoft Orleans provider architecture (https://dotnet.github.io/orleans/docs/implementation/provider-authoring/); OpenRouter provider routing (https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/routing/provider-selection); Microsoft ASP.NET Core dependency injection (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/dependency-injection).

